tdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank Street Bookstore (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Partnership for New York City', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-03T09:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Haag', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haag', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b402b95f-74c1-5e8e-8b52-d4fb91650a40', 'word_count': 1637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b402b95f-74c1-5e8e-8b52-d4fb91650a40'}, {'abstract': 'For weeks, I have been trying to understand my own tears in the presence of a dying creature I did not love.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/03/opinion/feral-cat-dying.html', 'snippet': 'For weeks, I have been trying to understand my own tears in the presence of a dying creature I did not love.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — I first saw it in bits — two paws here, the tip of a tail there — on grainy, black-and-white images taken by our backyard trail camera. Normally that device captures only the wild animals you’d expect to find in a first-ring suburb: opossums, mainly, but also rabbits and raccoons and rat snakes, sometimes an owl, now and then a fox. I once saw a bobcat slink across our street, but it’s never shown up in the trail-cam photos. My heart lifted when I first saw a clearly feline haunch in one of the pictures, but the scale was all wrong for a bobcat. It was just an ordinary house cat, prowling in my backyard.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/31/opinion/31renklWeb/31renklWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_nam