ey. But for many companies — from established businesses to smaller start-ups — the pandemic hasn’t prevented them from finding investors willing to open their wallets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Venture fund-raising is alive and (relatively) well.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2020-08-03T13:49:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40fe7c38-ca2c-560e-abe8-62f2ec37c8b4', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40fe7c38-ca2c-560e-abe8-62f2ec37c8b4'}, {'abstract': 'As the influenza pandemic swept across the United States in 1918 and 1919, masks took a role in political and cultural wars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/03/us/mask-protests-1918.html', 'snippet': 'As the influenza pandemic swept across the United States in 1918 and 1919, masks took a role in political and cultural wars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The masks were called muzzles, germ shields and dirt traps. They gave people a “pig-like snout.” Some people snipped holes in their masks to smoke cigars. Others fastened them to dogs in mockery. Bandits used them to rob banks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/16/multimedia/00xp-1918masks-03/00xp-1918masks-03-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/07/16/multimedia/00xp-1918masks-03/00xp-1918masks-03-article