, 'pub_date': '2020-08-03T21:17:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathleen Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fd5066b-6d9f-5fb8-adff-97edd34c706c', 'word_count': 1295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fd5066b-6d9f-5fb8-adff-97edd34c706c'}, {'abstract': 'The president sidestepped Congress when it became clear that his nominee for a top Defense Department position would not win Senate approval.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/03/us/politics/tata-pentagon.html', 'snippet': 'The president sidestepped Congress when it became clear that his nominee for a top Defense Department position would not win Senate approval.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — In making an end run around Congress to appoint Anthony J. Tata, a retired brigadier general with a history of Islamophobic and other inflammatory views, to a top Defense Department post, President Trump has once again put the military exactly where it does not want to be: in the middle of a political battle that could hurt bipartisan support for the Pentagon.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/30/us/politics/03dc-military/30dc-tata-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/07/30/us/politics/03dc-military/30dc-tata-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/30/us/politics/03dc-military/30dc-tata-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 's