exas', 'kicker': 'Fiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seeing Double', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Saint from Texas (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Edmund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-04T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Bird', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bird', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/80fa83ca-b714-5684-8bb8-bc57f8e0bc58', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80fa83ca-b714-5684-8bb8-bc57f8e0bc58'}, {'abstract': 'A memorial makes a statement about who is worth preserving. Why not a sacred and beautiful nothingness?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/04/opinion/us-monuments-rome-unknown-god.html', 'snippet': 'A memorial makes a statement about who is worth preserving. Why not a sacred and beautiful nothingness?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Rome’s Palatine Museum, built atop the excavation site of Emperor Domitian’s palace and not far from imperial residences of Tiberius and Augustus, there is a curious bit of masonry that was rediscovered in 1820. This artifact is exhibited alongside terra cotta heads of Jupiter and marble sculptures of Apollo, all of the grand detritus of a lost civilization housed in a former monastery. At the corner of one of the museum’s galleries sits this short altar chiseled from granite that’s slightly worn from the elements and framed with a modest curled design where a statue might sit. No idol is framed by the base; no statue of strong Jupiter or beautiful Apollo sits atop the altar. It is rather simply dedicated to Agnostos Theos — the “Unknown God.” Where a deity would normally be present is rather a sacred and beautiful nothingness.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/06/opinion/06stone/04stone-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/06/opinion/06stone/04stone-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 665}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/06/opinion/06stone/04stone-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 924, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/06/opinion/06stone/04stone-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1847, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/06/opinion/06stone/04stone-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/06/opinion/06stone/04stone-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/06/opinion/06stone/04stone-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'We Need a Monument to the Unknown America', 'kicker': 'THE STONE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roman Civilization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-04T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ed Simon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/95c9c198-1033-57ff-937c-34e9ae7e88b3', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95c9c198-1033-57ff-937c-34e9ae7e88b3'}, {'abstract': 'In “A Furious Sky,” Eric Jay Dolin recounts 500 years of reckoning with the monster storms that come in off the Atlantic Ocean.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/04/books/review/furious-sky-hurricanes-eric-jay-dolin.html', 'snippet': 'In “A Furious Sky,” Eric Jay Dolin recounts 500 years of reckoning with the monster storms that come in off the Atlantic Ocean.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A FURIOUS SKYThe Five-Hundred-Year History of America’s HurricanesBy Eric Jay Dolin', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/books/review/Kolbert/Kolbert-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/16/books/review/Kolbert/Kolbert-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 414}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/books/review/Kolbert/Kolbert-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 707, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'imag