dcasts', 'subsection_name': 'The Daily', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28b33cb9-0543-5703-b3ed-050d413d04ba', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28b33cb9-0543-5703-b3ed-050d413d04ba'}, {'abstract': 'He reminds us it’s hard to respect democratic political institutions while disdaining the founders of those institutions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/05/opinion/sunday/edmund-burke.html', 'snippet': 'He reminds us it’s hard to respect democratic political institutions while disdaining the founders of those institutions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Had it not been for the revolution in France, Edmund Burke would likely have been remembered, a bit vaguely, as an 18th-century philosopher-statesman of extravagant rhetorical gifts but frustratingly ambivalent views. The Irish-born member of the British Parliament was sympathetic to the grievances of the American colonies but not (like his onetime friend Thomas Paine) an enthusiastic champion of their independence; an acerbic critic of George III but a firm defender of monarchy; a staunch opponent of English rapacity in India but a supporter of British Empire; an advocate for the gradual emancipation of at least some slaves, but no believer in equality.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/09/opinion/sunday/07Stephens/07Stephens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 894, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/09/opinion/sunday/07Stephens/07Stephens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 894}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/09/opinion/sunday/07Stephens/07Stephens-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 688, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_