mes.com/2020/08/06/movies/the-secret-garden-review.html', 'snippet': 'This version, featuring Colin Firth as the haunted uncle, hits the same notes as the 1911 novel and previous films, and that’s fine for this uncertain moment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a year defined by surprise, the predictability of “The Secret Garden” — a new film adaptation of Frances Hodgson Burnett’s beloved 1911 novel — proves more charming than tedious.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06thesecretgarden/merlin_175178184_f03b3454-89d2-4d06-8b4a-4bc7424a20c0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06thesecretgarden/merlin_175178184_f03b3454-89d2-4d06-8b4a-4bc7424a20c0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06thesecretgarden/merlin_175178184_f03b3454-89d2-4d06-8b4a-4bc7424a20c0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06thesecretgarden/merlin_175178184_f03b3454-89d2-4d06-8b4a-4bc7424a20c0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06thesecretgarden/merlin_175178184_f03b3454-89d2-4d06-8b4a-4bc7424a20c0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06thesecretgarden/merlin_175178184_f03b3454-89d2-4d06-8b4a-4bc7424a20c0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06thesecretgarden/merlin_175178184_f03b3454-89d2-4d06-8b4a-4bc7424a20c0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Secret Garden’ Review: It’s as Lovely as You Remember', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Secret Garden', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Secret Garden (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Firth, Colin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burnett, Frances Hodgson', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thorne, Jack (1978- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Munden, Marc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-06T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lovia Gyarkye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lovia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gyarkye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/07f54fb1-3a2c-5e1e-b243-0c65a004539e', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07f54fb1-3a2c-5e1e-b243-0c65a004539e'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Rylance, Johnny Depp and Robert Pattinson star in this Ciro Guerra film adapted from a J.M. Coetzee novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/06/movies/waiting-for-the-barbarians-review.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Rylance, Johnny Depp and Robert Pattinson star in this Ciro Guerra film adapted from a J.M. Coetzee novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stark sight of a western-style military outpost in the middle of a whole lot of desert nothingness, where “Waiting for the Barbarians” begins, may remind cinephiles of the 1976 Valerio Zurlini film “The Desert of the Tartars.” It’s unclear whether the director of this picture, Ciro Guerra, meant the scene as a homage to Zurlini’s film, but he didn’t even need to. As it happens, J.M. Coetzee, whose novel of the same name inspired this film, was likely influenced by Dino Buzzati’s book “The Tartar Steppe,” the source for “Tartars.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06waitingfor-art/merlin_175261029_3bf7a51f-e90d-4417-9f10-41afdb98ae60-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06waitingfor-art/merlin_175261029_3bf7a51f-e90d-4417-9f10-41afdb98ae60-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06waitingfor-art/merlin_175261029_3bf7a51f-e90d-4417-9f10-41afdb98ae60-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06waitingfor-art/merlin_175261029_3bf7a51f-e90d-4417-9f10-41afdb98ae60-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1386, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06waitingfor-art/06waitingfor-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06waitingfor-art/06waitingfor-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06waitingfor-art/06waitingfor-art-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Waiting for the Barbarians’ Review: An Aestheticized Fable of Colonialism', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Waiting for  the Barbarians', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Waiting for the Barbarians (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guerra, Ciro (1981- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-06T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Kenny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c9dfcc8-1238-5ecc-8a9c-98c401675325', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c9dfcc8-1238-5ecc-8a9c-98c401675325'}, {'abstract': 'Melina León’s beautifully composed film follows a woman whose baby was stolen from her after child birth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/06/movies/song-without-a-name-review.html', 'snippet': 'Melina León’s beautifully composed film follows a woman whose baby was stolen from her after child birth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Peruvian film “Song Without a Name” sets a dramatic mood with gorgeous black-and-white photography and an original guitar score. The story, loosely based on true events, begins in 1988 with Georgina (Pamela Mendoza), a Quechua woman who sells potatoes in the outer limits of Lima. She responds to a notice for a clinic that helps pregnant women like herself deliver babies. But in the moments after she gives birth, her child is taken from her.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06songwithout/merlin_175178241_ba2ab76f-0747-4661-81b1-216854b33048-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06songwithout/merlin_175178241_ba2ab76f-0747-4661-81b1-216854b33048-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06songwithout/merlin_175178241_ba2ab76f-0747-4661-81b1-216854b33048-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06songwithout/merlin_175178241_ba2ab76f-0747-4661-81b1-216854b33048-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1280, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06songwithout/merlin_175178241_ba2ab76f-0747-4661-81b1-216854b33048-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06songwithout/merlin_175178241_ba2ab76f-0747-4661-81b1-216854b33048-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/06songwithout/merlin_175178241_ba2ab76f-0747-4661-81b1-216854b33048-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Song Without a Name’ Review: A Tragedy in the Peruvian Hillside', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Song Without  a Name', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leon, Melina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Song Without a Name (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-06T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teo Bugbee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bugbee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/62250341-81c9-50fe-b21b-6dc0f532f626', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62250341-81c9-50fe-b21b-6dc0f532f626'}, {'abstract': 'At the heart of this documentary from Marlon Johnson and Anne Flatté is a drum line in Louisville, Ky., that offers children a chance to engage with Black art and history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/06/movies/river-city-drumbeat-review.html', 'snippet': 'At the heart of this documentary from Marlon Johnson and Anne Flatté is a drum line in Louisville, Ky., that offers children a chance to engage with Black art and history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on in the beautiful documentary “River City Drumbeat,” two leaders from the River City Drum Corp teach elementary school students in Kentucky a drum lesson. Albert Shumake, a new father who has been involved with the corp since his childhood, inspires students by bringing them back to their elemental beginnings. He reminds the class that the first sound they heard was their mother’s heartbeat. As he speaks, his mentor Nardie White accompanies him on a drum, following Shumake’s lead with a solid and simple beat.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New 