r': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Protective Clothing and Gear', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island City (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Plaxall Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-07T04:12:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kaya Laterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Laterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37e40ba1-4e77-526c-a056-a056c8c5db80', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37e40ba1-4e77-526c-a056-a056c8c5db80'}, {'abstract': 'A sweet Japanese film comes to Mubi. And an Oscar-winning satire airs on HBO.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/07/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday-sweet-bean-and-jojo-rabbit.html', 'snippet': 'A sweet Japanese film comes to Mubi. And an Oscar-winning satire airs on HBO.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SWEET BEAN (2016) Stream on Mubi. Sentaro (Masatoshi Nagase) — a Japanese baker who serves pancake-like pastries called “dorayaki” — is struggling, and appears perpetually grumpy. Then along comes Tokue (Kirin Kiki), the fairy godmother of “an,” the sweet red bean paste in the center of dorayaki. Seemingly out of nowhere, Tokue approaches Sentaro’s bakery and tells him she wants to work for him. Sentaro is hesitant at first. But Tokue’s an has something special, he realizes, and the wisdom she has to give is equally magical. In his review for The New York Times, Glenn Kenny wrote that “the movie, beautifully shot and acted, earns its ultimate sense of hope by confronting real heartbreak head-on, and with compassion.” “Sweet Bean,” based on a novel by Durian Sukegawa, was written and directed by Naomi Kawase, and it debuted at the 2015 Cannes Film Festival.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/07tvcol-2/merlin_105642877_3bcc6df5-aa82-4748-b620-e28db28a3ac3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/07tvcol-2/merlin_105642877_3bcc6df5-aa82-4748-b620-e28db28a3ac3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/07tvcol-2/merlin_105642877_3bcc6df5-aa82-4748-b620-e28db28a3ac3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/07tvcol-2/merlin_105642877_3bcc6df5-aa82-4748-b620-e28db28a3ac3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/07tvcol-2/07tvcol-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/07tvcol-2/07tvcol-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/07/arts/07tvcol-2/07tvcol-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV