'news_desk': 'NewsDesk', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Schaverien', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaverien', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bacdfc42-1a36-51ed-a038-0723831394d4', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bacdfc42-1a36-51ed-a038-0723831394d4'}, {'abstract': 'Picking up trash makes the outdoors a more beautiful space to inhabit — for everyone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/07/at-home/newsletter.html', 'snippet': 'Picking up trash makes the outdoors a more beautiful space to inhabit — for everyone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Welcome. The At Home team got a lovely email recently from a woman in Brooklyn who lives near Prospect Park. One of her pandemic activities, she reported, is to go to the park to collect trash. She walks in one direction, gloved and masked, picking up detritus and filling a plastic bag. Then she walks back across the fields she’s made pristine. “It looks clean,” she wrote, “and I’m filled with happiness I can share by protecting this recreational area as a sanctuary during a pandemic.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/multimedia/20200807-athome-newsletter01/merlin_175384092_702c3ec6-483f-4847-b914-8dd7dc0bfbde-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/20/multimedia/20200807-athome-newsletter01/merlin_175384092_702c3ec6-483f-4847-b914-8dd7dc0bfbde-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/multimedia/20200807-athome-newsletter01/merlin_175384092_702c3ec6-483f-4847-b914-8dd7dc0bfbde-jumbo.jpg'