 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruchir Sharma', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruchir', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sharma', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e4a6179-167d-5a5a-83da-1e47b1d9906d', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e4a6179-167d-5a5a-83da-1e47b1d9906d'}, {'abstract': 'Just because you can’t go to the games doesn’t mean your summer has to be athlete-free.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/08/at-home/coronavirus-sports-podcasts.html', 'snippet': 'Just because you can’t go to the games doesn’t mean your summer has to be athlete-free.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even after months of postponed seasons and canceled events, sports leagues continue to struggle to rebound (at least, in the United States). For fans, this means that 2020 will most likely be a year with fewer games and without any in-person games. Fortunately, there’s a raft of podcasts that can give seasoned fans some content to fill that void — and these four shows are so entertaining and enlightening that even the most sports-disinterested person will probably enjoy tuning in. Find them in your listening app of choice.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/09/multimedia/09AHsportspodcasts/09AHsportspodcasts-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 532, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/09/multimedia/09AHsportspodcasts/09AHsportspodcasts-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 532}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/09/multimedia/09AHsportspodcasts/09AHsportspodcasts-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 909, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr