subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-08T18:34:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Billy Witz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Billy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29b5d1b0-64dd-5547-9009-d9c4ae17c5c5', 'word_count': 1406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29b5d1b0-64dd-5547-9009-d9c4ae17c5c5'}, {'abstract': 'Many sports have returned to play without fans. Golf is deciding a major championship without them. For players used to feeding off the crowd’s energy and reactions, it’s just not the same.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/08/sports/golf/pga-championship-scores.html', 'snippet': 'Many sports have returned to play without fans. Golf is deciding a major championship without them. For players used to feeding off the crowd’s energy and reactions, it’s just not the same.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — For three days of the P.G.A. Championship, golf’s first major of this discombobulated sports year, the world’s best players were introduced at the start of their rounds by a public-address announcer with no public to address.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/08/sports/08pga-silence1/merlin_175451451_8ad2e8f0-5341-49e7-9157-007692000fd0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/08/sports/08pga-silence1/merlin_175451451_8ad2e8f0-5341-49e7-9157-007692000fd0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'