major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mail Order Companies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lewis Barbecue (Charleston, SC, Restaurant)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-11T00:08:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddc007f0-92dd-51f9-8195-65e2b71f0804', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddc007f0-92dd-51f9-8195-65e2b71f0804'}, {'abstract': 'Campfire Pottery, in Portland, makes these serving dishes in an old mill factory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/10/dining/campfire-pottery-bowls.html', 'snippet': 'Campfire Pottery, in Portland, makes these serving dishes in an old mill factory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If your name is Camp, and you make pottery, Campfire has to be your brand. The company in Portland, Maine, has workshops in the Dana Warp Mill, a converted factory in Westbrook, just west of Portland, where Kristen Camp, who creates an array of lovely pieces, some in the Japanese raku tradition, for serving and showing. Her husband, Joe Camp, is her partner in the business. Their simple, tactile new loop-handled bowl begs to be filled with summer’s ripest fruit, bright flowers or a tangle of salad. It also comes in charcoal gray.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10BurnerCamp01/10BurnerCamp01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 509, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10BurnerCamp01/10BurnerCamp01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 509}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10BurnerCamp01/10BurnerCamp01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 869, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10BurnerCamp01/10BurnerCamp01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1738, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10BurnerCamp01/10BurnerCamp01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10BurnerCamp01/10BurnerCamp01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10BurnerCamp01/10BurnerCamp01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Elegant Bowl Handmade in Maine', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Serve: An Elegant Bowl  Handmade in Maine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tableware', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Portland (Me)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campfire Studio LLC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-11T00:20:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1323374b-2d32-5e20-a815-38adfe434abb', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1323374b-2d32-5e20-a815-38adfe434abb'}, {'abstract': 'The Yocha Dehe Wintun Nation has just released a new  offering under its Séka Hills label.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/10/dining/seka-hills-olive-oil.html', 'snippet': 'The Yocha Dehe Wintun Nation has just released a new  offering under its Séka Hills label.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in 2011, the Yocha Dehe Wintun Nation in Northern California began bottling oil from arbequina olives its members were cultivating on their land. Now they have built a mill, opened two tasting rooms and expanded the acreage of olive trees from 82 to more than 500. They’ve added more single varietal bottlings of frantoio, picual and taggiasca, and earned extra-virgin status for their oils, which are used by a number of restaurants, mostly in California. Their new Tribal Blend is made from a selection of oils, from five estates in the Capay Valley, chosen by tribal citizens and the Séka Hills team. The blend is a bright chartreuse, mellow and rich with a piquant finish. The tribe grows other crops, including grapes for their wines, and say it is doing well despite the virus; the tribe’s casino resort, which follows safety protocols and social-distancing rules, just reopened, too.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10BurnerOil01/10BurnerOil01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/12/dining/10Burner