n most construction projects, concrete is responsible for about 8 percent of global carbon emissions. Several companies are working to create a greener mix.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On any given day, Central Concrete, in San Jose, Calif., does what concrete companies have been doing for centuries: combining sand, gravel, water and cement to create the slurry that is used in construction.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Concrete, a Centuries-Old Material, Gets a New Recipe', 'kicker': 'Square Feet', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Fixture of Construction Gets a Lot Greener', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concrete', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Building (Construction)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate (Commercial)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-11T09:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Margolies', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Margolies', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e91a886-aafa-56ef-adf8-ecfe047070a4', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e91a886-aafa-56ef-adf8-ecfe047070a4'}, {'abstract': 'The Sagaponack, N.Y., house was (sort of) affordable, but it needed a lot of work — and there wasn’t a lot of time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2020/08/11/realestate/revamping-a-simple