name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-12T07:43:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Express', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Victor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Victor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1744ee2e-e00e-53b5-88a4-9315f298c793', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1744ee2e-e00e-53b5-88a4-9315f298c793'}, {'abstract': 'The philosopher Kate Manne’s new book is about the ways in which male entitlement leads to a wide range of misogynistic behavior.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/12/books/review-entitled-how-male-privilege-hurts-women-kate-manne.html', 'snippet': 'The philosopher Kate Manne’s new book is about the ways in which male entitlement leads to a wide range of misogynistic behavior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a curious new refrain on the right — the repurposing of an old slogan from their opponents in the abortion fight to explain why people shouldn’t have to wear masks during a pandemic. “I thought it was my body, my choice!” the young Trump supporter Charlie Kirk recently declared on his podcast, a few days before an 80-year-old mentor of his died of complications from Covid-19.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/books/12BOOKMANNE1/12BOOKMANNE1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 850, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/13/books/12BOOKMANNE1/12BOOKMANNE1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 850}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/books/12BOOKMANNE1/12BOOKMANNE1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/books/12BOOKMANNE1/12BOOKMANNE1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1447, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/books/12BOOKMANNE1/12BOOKMANNE1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/13/books/12BOOKMANNE1/12BOOKMANNE1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/books/12BOOKMANNE1/12BOOKMANNE1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Entitled’ Takes a Scalpel to What Men Feel They Automatically Deserve', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking a Scalpel to Male Entitlement', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Entitled: How Male Privilege Hurts Women (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manne, Kate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-12T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Szalai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Szalai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7de81f0e-09ee-561e-a48c-67580d59ebec', 'word_count': 977, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7de81f0e-09ee-561e-a48c-67580d59ebec'}, {'abstract': 'Is it any surprise that when survival became a theme of daily life, I immediately thought of making cultured butter?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/12/magazine/the-spiritual-power-of-fat.html', 'snippet': 'Is it any surprise that when survival became a theme of daily life, I immediately thought of making cultured butter?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the pandemic came to my city and I understood that I was about to be spending an enormous amount of time in my apartment, I thought of the German artist Joseph Beuys. The tedium of lockdown finds an analogue in his 1974 performance “I Like America and America Likes Me,” in which he shut himself in a SoHo art gallery for three days with a live coyote, some straw, dozens of copies of The Wall Street Journal and a heap of wool felt. Beuys understood that the animal imperative of survival is never far from our lives, even when that imperative is tidied and civilized almost beyond recognition.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-LOR-1/16mag-LOR-1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-LOR-1/16mag-LOR-1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 437}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-LOR-1/16mag-LOR-1-jumbo.png', 'height': 745, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-LOR-1/16mag-LOR-1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1490, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-LOR-1/16mag-LOR-1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-LOR-1/16mag-LOR-1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-LOR-1/16mag-LOR-1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Spiritual Power of Fat', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cultured Butter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Butter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oils and Fats', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beuys, Joseph', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-12T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Kleeman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kleeman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2018573-0fbe-57b2-873a-9e40e11c4591', 'word_count': 949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2018573-0fbe-57b2-873a-9e40e11c4591'}, {'abstract': '‘The Notorious R.B.G.’ memes hit like relics as the symbol of progressive change looks more like a regular person for whom justice was not served.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/12/magazine/the-fandom-around-rbg-is-out-of-step-with-reality.html', 'snippet': '‘The Notorious R.B.G.’ memes hit like relics as the symbol of progressive change looks more like a regular person for whom justice was not served.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg revealed last month that she had been treated for cancerous lesions on her liver, a YouTube video whispered across the liberal internet like a prayer. The video, titled “Hang On Ruthie!” is a 2018 parody of the McCoys’ 1965 hit “Hang On Sloopy,” and every few months, it is resurrected through boomer chain emails and Facebook pages with names like the Rude Liberals. Filmed “somewhere in Oregon” by the musical duo Buffalo Romeo, “Hang On Ruthie!” features the earnestly progressive trappings of the Pacific Northwest (a local lesbian choir sings backup in black judicial robes) and a charmingly amateurish production (still photographs of Supreme Court justices are made to wink or bob their heads to the beat). The “Sloopy” lyrics are recast to celebrate Ginsburg’s legal prowess: “You know you argue so good/You know your briefs are so tight/You got the juris and the prudence/Keep counsel up all night.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-screenland/16mag-screenland-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-screenland/16mag-screenland-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-screenland/16mag-screenland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-screenland/16mag-screenland-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-screenland/16mag-screenland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-screenland/16mag-screenland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-screenland/16mag-screenland-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fandom Around R.B.G. Is Out of Step With Reality', 'kicker': 'Screenland', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Justice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ginsburg, Ruth Bader', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting Rights Act (1965)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-12T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Hess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/adb7a655-abab-571c-bc61-c9493c35323b', 'word_count': 1250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/adb7a655-abab-571c-bc61-c9493c35323b'}, {'abstract': 'The moments spent watching and waiting for this chocolate-flake raspberry ice cream to form capture a childish delight that we could all use right now.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/12/magazine/the-particular-texture-and-joy-of-homemade-ice-cream.html', 'snippet': 'The moments spent watching and waiting for this chocolate-flake raspberry ice cream to form capture a childish delight that we could all use right now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Because my husband, Michael, and I lived in the same neighborhood in Brooklyn before we met, we share memories that go back to our childhoods. We remember when there was a Woolworth’s on Avenue J — it was where I bought candy with my weekly allowance. We remember blackout cake from Ebinger’s and pizza from DiFara, now famous but then the local slice joint. So one recent night, when we were finishing dinner and Michael said, “I was thinking about the ice cream at that restaurant near Coney Island Avenue,” I jumped in and said, “Remember how it always had itsy bits of ice in it!” It was just the point he wanted to make. The ice was always there, but somehow still always unexpected. I liked it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-eat-02/16mag-eat-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-eat-02/16mag-eat-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 419}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-eat-02/16mag-eat-02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-eat-02/16mag-eat-02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1431, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-eat-02/16mag-eat-02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-eat-02/16mag-eat-02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-eat-02/16mag-eat-02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Particular Texture and Joy of Homemade Ice Cream', 'kicker': 'Eat ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Memories of Ice Cream', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ice Cream and Frozen Desserts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chocolate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Raspberries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-12T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dorie Greenspan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dorie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenspan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/977ff8cc-fe69-5a85-887d-88926d460f63', 'word_count': 855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/977ff8cc-fe69-5a85-887d-88926d460f63'}, {'abstract': 'Early risers did the equivalent of walking 20 to 30 minutes more each day compared to “evening types,” who tend to stay up late.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/12/well/move/whether-you-are-a-night-owl-or-early-bird-may-affect-how-much-you-move.html', 'snippet': 'Early risers did the equivalent of walking 20 to 30 minutes more each day compared to “evening types,” who tend to stay up late.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People who are evening types go to bed later and wake up later than morning types. They also tend to move around far less throughout the day, according to an interesting new study of how our innate body clocks may be linked to our physical activity habits. The study, one of the first to objectively track daily movements of a large sample of early birds and night owls, suggests that knowing our chronotype might be important for our health.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/18/well/physed-chronotype/physed-chronotype-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/18/well/physed-chronotype/physed-chronotype-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/18/well/physed-chronotype/physed-chronotype-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/18/well/physed-chronotype/physed-chronotype-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/18/well/p