ganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c9d640f-185c-558e-b11b-abff41117d11', 'word_count': 1641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c9d640f-185c-558e-b11b-abff41117d11'}, {'abstract': 'Amy Poehler’s character on “Parks and Recreation” is a vindication for principled, chatty ladies everywhere, even if her idealism feels impossible nowadays.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/12/arts/television/parks-and-recreation-amy-poehler.html', 'snippet': 'Amy Poehler’s character on “Parks and Recreation” is a vindication for principled, chatty ladies everywhere, even if her idealism feels impossible nowadays.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I didn’t like Leslie Knope at first. Then again, who did? Leslie, played by Amy Poehler, was the heroine of “Parks and Recreation,” a half-hour comedy on NBC that debuted in 2009 to aggressively middling reviews. Most write-ups shrugged it off as a mockumentary little sister to “The Office,” with which it shared a creative team. Even critics who liked it side-eyed Leslie. “Deluded” came up a lot when they described her. So did “ditsy.” The New York Times compared her to the “women who volunteer to look foolish on reality shows.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/00summer-soloski/merlin_175373424_18a8ca7e-c617-401c-af48-d21197ff35de-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/00summer-soloski/merlin_175373424_18a8ca7e-c617-401c-af48-d21197ff35de-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/00summer-soloski/merlin_175373424_18a8ca7e-c617-401c-af48-d21197ff35de-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 770, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/00summer-soloski/merlin_175373424_18a8ca7e-c617-401c-af48-d21197ff35de-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1541, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/00summer-soloski/00summer-soloski-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/00summer-soloski/00summer-soloski-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/00summer-soloski/00summer-soloski-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I’m a Type-A Know-It-All. Leslie Knope Showed Me That’s a Good Thing.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leslie Knope &amp; Me', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poehler, Amy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation (TV Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-12T14:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87daf9b7-b694-51d9-b964-29292763854e', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87daf9b7-b694-51d9-b964-29292763854e'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs of generations of Black suffragists offer invaluable documents about their thwarted and central roles in the history of women’s rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/12/arts/19th-amendment-black-womens-suffrage-photos.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs of generations of Black suffragists offer invaluable documents about their thwarted and central roles in the history of women’s rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the last day of his life, Frederick Douglass attended a meeting of the National Women’s Council. The prodigious orator and abolitionist came home to Cedar Hill, his hilltop house in the Anacostia neighborhood of Washington, D.C.; spoke with his wife at the time, Helen Pitts Douglass, about the events and future plans; clasped his hands to his chest and died that evening. It seemed impossible that such an indomitable man was gone.', 'print_section': 'TW', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Black Suffragists, the Lens Was a Mighty Sword', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Photos as Civic Weapons for Black Activists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteenth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fifteenth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation and Desegregation', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-12T14:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SpecialSections', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Elizabeth Lewis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Elizabeth', 'lastname': 'Lewis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e428f40-6f3b-536a-8295-1b0bd6e28f9e', 'word_count': 1268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e428f40-6f3b-536a-8295-1b0bd6e28f9e'}, {'abstract': 'The show will be filmed in the empty Broadway theater where it had been playing in previews, and still plans to open in May 2021.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/12/theater/diana-musical-netflix.html', 'snippet': 'The show will be filmed in the empty Broadway theater where it had been playing in previews, and still plans to open in May 2021.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With Broadway still shuttered, Netflix is doing for the theater community what it and other streaming services have done for the movie business: airing content that can’t be seen where it was initially intended. On Wednesday, the Los Gatos-based company announced that it will stream a specially filmed version of “Diana: A Musical,” a new show about the British princess, ahead of its debut on Broadway.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/12diana1/merlin_170122683_e0262ef0-8dab-41af-a3f8-1d5d9f9c194c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/13/arts/12diana1/merlin_170122683_e0262ef0-8dab-41af