'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Simmons Bedding Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-13T09:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marisa Meltzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meltzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31619ffb-01c8-5ab0-8163-86f9e9a7665d', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31619ffb-01c8-5ab0-8163-86f9e9a7665d'}, {'abstract': 'A poem that reminds us there is always more to come.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/13/magazine/poem-my-father-disappears-into-flowers.html', 'snippet': 'A poem that reminds us there is always more to come.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Poetry forever grants us leaps and blurs. Sometimes it’s not enough to be where we are. Sometimes we need to be everywhere: present with the lost, held by transient blossoms. Jan Beatty’s new poems in “The Body Wars” shimmer with luminous connection, travel a big life and grand map of encounters. Deeper breath feels present in the poem’s original uses of punctuation — stanzas, even a last one, ending with a colon. More to come? Always more. Most important, “he’s not gone” floats by itself, the truth a daughter tunes to. Selected by Naomi Shihab Nye', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-poem-1/16mag-poem-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/16/magazine/16mag-poem-1/16mag-poem-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 637}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/m