: 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d5d6b75-537a-549a-b8fc-f981690c4dc6', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d5d6b75-537a-549a-b8fc-f981690c4dc6'}, {'abstract': 'Cartoonists used the press to battle anti-suffrage stereotypes and create a new image for the movement to enfranchise women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/14/us/suffrage-cartoons.html', 'snippet': 'Cartoonists used the press to battle anti-suffrage stereotypes and create a new image for the movement to enfranchise women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Alice Paul recruited a cartoonist for her newspaper The Suffragist in 1914, the idea of that activist was already sketched in the public mind — and it was not pretty. Throughout the 19th century and into the 20th, Americans had encountered countless images showing women suffragists as old, mannish and unattractive. In pursuing the vote, women were portrayed as threatening national values, the sanctity of the home and their husbands’ masculinity.', 'print_section': 'TW', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/us/11suffrage-cartoons-04/11suffrage-cartoons-04-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 861, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/16/us/11suffrage-cartoons-04/11suffrage-cartoons-04-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 861}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/us/11suffrage-cartoons-04/11suffrage-cartoons-04-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima