ation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45e533f8-a34a-536e-9165-3b6b934d36a1', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45e533f8-a34a-536e-9165-3b6b934d36a1'}, {'abstract': 'Marilyn Stasio finds much to like in the latest batch of crime novels, filled with abrasive, unpleasant sorts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/14/books/review/crime-fiction-stasio-jeff-abbott-never-ask-me.html', 'snippet': 'Marilyn Stasio finds much to like in the latest batch of crime novels, filled with abrasive, unpleasant sorts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Plucky heroines make me queasy. (It takes so much energy to be plucky.) The protagonist of Katie Tallo’s first novel, DARK AUGUST (HarperCollins, 448 pp., paper; $16.99), is better than that. Augusta (Gus) Monet is smart and resourceful, just too stubborn to know when to quit. When she returns to her hometown — Elgin, Ontario, “a town that once was but is no more” — her instinct is to burn it down. But “greed and spite and toxic wastewater” from fracking have taken care of that already. So her only business here is to claim the house (and the old dog who comes with it) left to her by her great-grandmother.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/books/review/16Crime/16Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/16/books/review/16Crime/16Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 896}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/books/review/16Crime/16Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 686, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur