n': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1322827b-b063-5b67-8497-932759f8ded9', 'word_count': 1076, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1322827b-b063-5b67-8497-932759f8ded9'}, {'abstract': 'Amid the real-life terrors of a pandemic, the writer takes solace in this long-running British crime series, which may be the only TV drama whose title character is a corpse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/14/arts/television/silent-witness-bbc.html', 'snippet': 'Amid the real-life terrors of a pandemic, the writer takes solace in this long-running British crime series, which may be the only TV drama whose title character is a corpse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It may sound perverse, if not masochistic, to say that I’ve spent weeks of quarantine watching autopsies. But these procedures have been the ultimate escape: Taking place in a distant, fictional environment, they usually involve an agent of death that isn’t a volatile, out-of-control virus but a volatile, out-of-control human being. (At least a relentless person’s range is limited.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/14/arts/14comfort-silent-4/merlin_175577997_00c7e54c-0537-44fa-b897-87d190ce6ce9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/14/arts/14comfort-silent-4/merlin_175577997_00c7e54c-0537-44fa-b897-87d190ce6ce9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/14/arts/14comfort-silent-4/merlin_175577997_00c7e54c-0537-44fa-b897-87d190ce6ce9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '