Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/53fdba34-2e09-5120-b3aa-9bd7a172f521', 'word_count': 1213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53fdba34-2e09-5120-b3aa-9bd7a172f521'}, {'abstract': 'The sash has had an improbable journey, worn by both suffragists and bachelorettes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/15/opinion/sashes-suffrage-19th-amendment.html', 'snippet': 'The sash has had an improbable journey, worn by both suffragists and bachelorettes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A sash — a piece of fabric draped over the head and worn from the shoulder to the opposite hip — is a strange garment. Its purpose is, essentially, decorative, but it has been worn for decoration by many types: It was part of male military dress uniforms in Europe at least as long ago as the 16th century. Royalty make use of them as well — you may recall that at his 2011 wedding, Prince William wore a bright blue sash with his dress uniform, and Princess Grace of Monaco frequently appeared in hers, an Order of St. Charles band.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/opinion/sunday/16friedman/16friedman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/16/opinion/sunday/16friedman/16friedman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/opinion/sunday/16friedman/16friedman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/16/opinion/sunday/16friedman/16friedman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1434, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':