 Reid J. Epstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Weiyi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Reid', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Epstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/f860bd8d-cf8d-5daf-9950-b7966a5a8988', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/f860bd8d-cf8d-5daf-9950-b7966a5a8988'}, {'abstract': 'A corporate-owned team runs against everything most German soccer fans hold dear. So how do the club’s most ardent supporters square their beliefs with their passion?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/18/sports/soccer/champions-league-leipzig-psg.html', 'snippet': 'A corporate-owned team runs against everything most German soccer fans hold dear. So how do the club’s most ardent supporters square their beliefs with their passion?', 'lead_paragraph': 'RB Leipzig’s executives have long since grown used to the protests. Some are eerie: Union Berlin’s most ardent fans dressed in black plastic and stood in silence for the first 15 minutes of a game between the teams. Some seem a tad petty: Borussia Dortmund still refuses to use Leipzig’s crest when the team visits Signal Iduna Park. And, occasionally, they get a little gory: Dynamo Dresden fans once greeted RB Leipzig by throwing a severed bull’s head onto the field.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/17/sports/17onsoccer1/merlin_170314839_7d4cdf15-546e-4eb5-acfa-f2af0342cdd3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/17/sports/17onsoccer1/merlin_170314839_7d4cdf15-546e-4eb5-acfa-f2af0342cdd3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xl