ubsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Weiyi Cai and Reid J. Epstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Weiyi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Reid', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Epstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/af1a2bbc-ef28-5db3-acc4-b18eda8f90f6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/af1a2bbc-ef28-5db3-acc4-b18eda8f90f6'}, {'abstract': 'Jonathan Swan confronted Donald Trump with unwelcome facts and revealed what he is willing to acknowledge as true.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/19/magazine/trump-axios-interview.html', 'snippet': 'Jonathan Swan confronted Donald Trump with unwelcome facts and revealed what he is willing to acknowledge as true.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a Tuesday in late July, Jonathan Swan, a political reporter for Axios, conducted an interview with President Trump at the White House. Nearly five months into the coronavirus pandemic, Trump’s incompetent response was by then an inarguable historical fact, though Trump had done what he could to deflect blame. “I don’t take responsibility at all,” he said in March, regarding problems with U.S. testing. Since then he had pointed fingers at state governors, Democrats in Congress and the Chinese Communist Party.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/23/magazine/23mag-screenland/23mag-screenland-articleLarge.png', 'height': 208, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/23/magazine/23mag-screenland/23mag-screenland-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 208}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype