umbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/arts/19nasser1/merlin_175806666_e3ca9f88-d430-49bd-bf8e-a9257a04d7d5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/arts/19nasser1/19nasser1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/20/arts/19nasser1/19nasser1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/arts/19nasser1/19nasser1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Latif Nasser, Harvard Ph.D., on the Rewards of Being Dumb', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'His Harvard Ph.D.? A License to Be Dumb', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nasser, Latif', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Radiolab (Radio Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Connected (TV Program)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-19T16:50:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Remy Tumin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Remy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tumin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46845bc0-d567-5628-87ca-396e02604d7a', 'word_count': 1317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46845bc0-d567-5628-87ca-396e02604d7a'}, {'abstract': 'Sascha Dornhöfer and Alexandra Rothert live with and by their art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/19/t-magazine/berlin-infamous-skating-couple-design.html', 'snippet': 'Sascha Dornhöfer and Alexandra Rothert live with and by their art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The key to rhythm skating — a soul-based style that combines roller skating and dance — is to master a series of shuffle skate moves with names like Crazy Legs, Shoot the Duck and the Grapevine. While the majority of participants like to skate or “battle” in public spaces, Sascha Dornhöfer, 50, and Alexandra Rothert, 48, treat the sport as an art practice. They call themselves the Infamous Skating Couple and perform in self-produced video works or in galleries, including, most recently, Berlin’s König Galerie. The pair makes a point of honoring the originators of the discipline, but they also strive to push it in new directions. “We want skating to be taken seriously, to be seen as progressive,” says Dornhöfer. “Which is why we perform alone, to experimental music, in dystopian settings, and we never smile.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/18/t-magazine/07tmag-berlin-slide-0TTL/07tmag-berlin-slide-0TTL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/18/t-magazine/07tmag-berlin-slide-0TTL/07tmag-berlin-slide-0TTL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',