'The Supreme Court announced Wednesday that it would hear arguments in the latest case seeking to invalidate the Affordable Care Act — one backed by President Trump — on Nov. 10, a week after the presidential election.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Supreme Court will hear a suit seeking to overturn Obamacare a week after the election.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2020-08-19T19:02:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Elections', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87437792-0c1d-523d-b70b-55b943cf91a1', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87437792-0c1d-523d-b70b-55b943cf91a1'}, {'abstract': 'Both are threatening to pull out of California this week over a law requiring them to treat their workers as full-fledged employees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/19/opinion/uber-lyft-shutting-down-california.html', 'snippet': 'Both are threatening to pull out of California this week over a law requiring them to treat their workers as full-fledged employees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ride-hailing apps Uber and Lyft have long disingenuously insisted that they are not transportation companies. This is a legal strategy that, so far, has allowed them to label their legions of drivers contract workers, depriving them of company-backed benefits like health care, paid leave and severance pay.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/19/opinion/19bensinger