ginal': 'By Emily Cochrane and Michael Levenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cochrane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Levenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/911d848a-25ce-5227-987d-1616d8f27b1b', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/911d848a-25ce-5227-987d-1616d8f27b1b'}, {'abstract': 'Nicolas Ghesquière presents a new, if retro-feeling, take on Louis Vuitton’s signature monogram.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/20/t-magazine/louis-vuitton-speedy-bag.html', 'snippet': 'Nicolas Ghesquière presents a new, if retro-feeling, take on Louis Vuitton’s signature monogram.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some logos move in and out of favor with the seasons. Then there’s Louis Vuitton’s signature monogram canvas, with its interlaced L and V flanked by various geometric flowers, which seems to be another thing entirely, one impervious to trends and maybe even time itself. In fact, the monogram hasn’t always been around, nor does it date to the house’s founding in 1854, when Louis Vuitton, who had previously been a packer for Empress Eugénie, the wife of Napoleon III, began selling travel trunks out of a store on Paris’s Rue Neuve-des-Capucines, just off Place Vendôme. His go-to motif was a waterproof gray canvas. The monogram came into existence just over four decades later, when Louis’s son, Georges, took inspiration from the kitchen tiles of the family’s home in Asnières in north-central France. The house, built in 1859, was decorated in the Art Nouveau style, and the tile pattern featured a trio of flowers outlined by a clover that was in turn set within a circle, as well as diamonds with petal-like points. Another reference may have been the Gothic Revival then sweeping through France — when seen through that lens, the monogram can’t help but recall architectural quatrefoils carved out of stone or glass in the era’s great cathedrals. What is certain is that, in designing it, Georges was acting in honor of his father, who had died four years prior, leaving the brand’s legacy in his son’s hands.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-lv/20tmag-lv-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-lv/20tmag-lv-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 480}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-lv/20tmag-lv-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-lv/20tmag-lv-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1638, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-lv/20tmag-lv-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-lv/20tmag-lv-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-lv/20tmag-lv-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Longstanding and Most Iconic Motif Is Reimagined', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reimagining a Classic; At Vuitton, Honoring Past and Present', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louis Vuitton', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghesquiere, Nicolas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-20T17:23:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Remsen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Remsen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ea10403-5141-57e3-978f-865352aa2dab', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ea10403-5141-57e3-978f-865352aa2dab'}, {'abstract': 'An African-American regiment revolts against a racist police force in Kevin Willmott’s World War I drama.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/20/movies/the-24th-review.html', 'snippet': 'An African-American regiment revolts against a racist police force in Kevin Willmott’s World War I drama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Set in Jim Crow-era Texas during World War I, Kevin Willmott’s “The 24th” dramatizes real-life events so inherently appalling — and so presently relevant — that we should never feel less than engaged. Yet this stultifyingly earnest movie makes its points with such a heavy hand that its horrors struggle to resonate.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/arts/twentyfourth2/merlin_175721226_ad383b2a-68dc-4688-b9f5-b1f7baee2c92-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/20/arts/twentyfour