', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-20T21:16:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/128dadda-dd58-57cb-8d33-3d679f2e36c5', 'word_count': 1232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/128dadda-dd58-57cb-8d33-3d679f2e36c5'}, {'abstract': 'Late last month, as the Texas Republican Party was shifting into campaign mode, it unveiled a new slogan, lifting a rallying cry straight from a once-unthinkable source: the internet-driven conspiracy theory known as QAnon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/20/us/elections/the-republican-embrace-of-qanon-goes-far-beyond-trump.html', 'snippet': '', 'lead_paragraph': 'Late last month, as the Texas Republican Party was shifting into campaign mode, it unveiled a new slogan, lifting a rallying cry straight from a once-unthinkable source: the internet-driven conspiracy theory known as QAnon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/us/politics/20-live-qanon/merlin_175618101_3412ea68-f476-494b-bf39-4360122ab097-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/20/us/politics/20-live-qanon/merlin_175618101_3412ea68-f476-494b-bf39-4360122ab097-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/us/politics/20-live-qanon/merlin_175618101_3412ea68-f476-494b-bf39-4360122ab097-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/20/us/politics