 'New York City', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-25T09:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffery C. Mays', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffery', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Mays', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/439f1f47-1884-5612-965c-7d2bdfe75bcc', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/439f1f47-1884-5612-965c-7d2bdfe75bcc'}, {'abstract': 'The Republican Party may not have a platform, but it has a plan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/25/opinion/trump-convention-platform.html', 'snippet': 'The Republican Party may not have a platform, but it has a plan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republicans chose not to produce a platform for their convention, no statement of values or declaration of principle. Instead, the party has approved a resolution to “enthusiastically support” President Trump’s “America-first agenda,” whatever that may be. And while the White House has produced a bullet-point outline of its second-term agenda, this week’s convention itself has little content planned other than cultural grievance and worshipful praise for the president. As one veteran congressional aide told Politico, the only thing Republicans believe now is “Owning the libs and pissing off the media.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/25/opinion/sunday/25Bouie/25Bouie-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/25/opinion/sunday/25Bouie/25Bouie-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image