'lead_paragraph': 'Gail Sheehy, a star journalist at New York magazine and a writer of creative nonfiction who examined cultural shifts and societal trends, in part by exploring the interior lives of public figures, died on Monday at 83. Her most famous book, “Passages” (1976), about the stages of a person’s development from early adulthood to midlife crisis and beyond, was just one of 17 books she wrote to give a reader a greater awareness of life, and his or her own place in it. Below are reviews of some of her most notable works.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/25/books/25sheehy-books/25sheehy-books-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/25/books/25sheehy-books/25sheehy-books-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/25/books/25sheehy-books/25sheehy-books-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/25/books/25sheehy-books/25sheehy-books-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/25/books/25sheehy-books/25sheehy-books-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/25/books/25sheehy-books/25sheehy-books-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/25/books/25sheehy-books/25sheehy-books-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gail Sheehy’s Books Helped Readers Define Their Lives', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheehy, Gail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-25T18:58:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Jordan and Amisha Padnani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jordan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Amisha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Padnani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec21eb3c-d76d-5795-bff3-eaed57d46059', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec21eb3c-d76d-5795-bff3-eaed57d46059'}, {'abstract': 'America can’t go back to being everywhere and solving every problem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/25/opinion/biden-foreign-policy.html', 'snippet': 'America can’t go back to being everywhere and solving every problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON —\xa0When Joe Biden was nominated as the Democratic Party’s presidential candidate last week, he committed not only to rebuilding America, but also to “build it back better.” His campaign is selling a promise: Soon President Trump will be gone and the United States can return to the normalcy of pre-Trump life, slightly improved. This is as true of foreign policy as of anything else.',