 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/930878c3-ddf9-54ca-8786-0ab549c56dbf', 'word_count': 948, 'uri': 'nyt://article/930878c3-ddf9-54ca-8786-0ab549c56dbf'}, {'abstract': 'The 1873 murders of dozens of former slaves in a flyspeck Louisiana town still reverberate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/28/opinion/black-lives-civil-rights.html', 'snippet': 'The 1873 murders of dozens of former slaves in a flyspeck Louisiana town still reverberate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An hour after sunset on Easter Sunday in 1873, a stern-wheel riverboat put ashore at Colfax, La., a ramshackle settlement surrounded by cotton plantations on the east bank of the Red River. Rain was falling. As passengers disembarked, they found themselves stumbling in the dark over what turned out to be the lifeless bodies of African-Americans who had been freed from slavery eight years earlier at the conclusion of the Civil War.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/29/opinion/28krakauer/28krakauer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/29/opinion/28krakauer/28krakauer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/29/opinion/28krakauer/28krakauer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/29/opinion/28krakauer/28krakauer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1325, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/29/opinion/28krakauer/28krakauer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/08/29/opinion/28krakauer/28krakauer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/29/opinion/28krakauer/28krakauer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Massacre That Emboldened White Supremacists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Legacy of a Racist Massacre', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colfax (La)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reconstruction Era', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thirteenth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fourteenth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ku Klux Klan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-28T23:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Briggs and Jon Krakauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Briggs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krakauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d67f0a82-3f24-50df-b8b2-eefb6d9951fc', 'word_count': 1301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d67f0a82-3f24-50df-b8b2-eefb6d9951fc'}, {'abstract': 'Both the Republican and Democratic events reflected the odd mashup of our current media moment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/28/opinion/sunday/republican-democratic-conventions.html', 'snippet': 'Both the Republican and Democratic events reflected the odd mashup of our current media moment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The faces flicker across our screens, making a pitch in speech and song to voters who may or may not be listening. To document the national political conventions in this pandemic year, Damon Winter went into ordinary people’s homes in upstate New York and projected images from the live broadcasts across bedrooms, living rooms, kitchens, basements — wherever the residents typically watch or listen to the news. The photographs he took of these projections capture the strangeness of this year’s conventions, and how dramatically they differed from the events in a typical year.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/30/opinion/28omarawinter1/merlin_176100324_2804282e-efaf-46ae-b2c7-51bb03b507ab-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/08/30/opinion/28omarawinter1/merlin_176100324_2804282e-efaf-46ae-b2c7-51bb03b507ab-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt