 '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7904b31e-1ce7-5e02-894b-26f96f1965a9', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7904b31e-1ce7-5e02-894b-26f96f1965a9'}, {'abstract': 'China’s new export rules, which could delay or scuttle a sale of TikTok, have further turned giant companies into pawns in a geopolitical struggle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/31/technology/tiktok-export-us-china.html', 'snippet': 'China’s new export rules, which could delay or scuttle a sale of TikTok, have further turned giant companies into pawns in a geopolitical struggle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — On most days, companies like ByteDance, Microsoft, Walmart and Oracle are considered kings within their fields. But over the weekend, it became increasingly clear that they can also be something else: a set of pawns.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TikTok Deal Faces Complications as U.S. and China Ratchet Up Tit-for-Tat', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. and China Battle Over TikTok', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TikTok (ByteDance)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corp', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walmart Stores Inc', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oracle Corporation', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-08-31T21:58:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Isaac and Ana Swanson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swanson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e35259f-e1a2-544c-a07a-65ee37cd33b1', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e35259f-e1a2-544c-a07a-65ee37cd33b1'}, {'abstract': 'He inspired a generation of young, Black players when their acceptance was not taken for granted. He pushed open doors for plenty of coaches, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/31/sports/ncaabasketball/john-thompson-georgetown-ewing.html', 'snippet': 'He inspired a generation of young, Black players when their acceptance was not taken for granted. He pushed open doors for plenty of coaches, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The story, as John Thompson Jr. often shared it, was that he was told by the Georgetown president, upon being hired to coach the university’s moribund men’s basketball program in the spring of 1972, that it would be great if he could win a few games. Maybe even qualify for a slice of postseason affirmation in the National Invitation Tournament.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/08/31/sports/31onbasketball-thompson1/merlin_176407266_206f0367-bb1d-4748-8faf-3232534d39aa-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_nam